const CATALOG = {"openings": [{"id": 1, "name": "The Epistolary or \"Found Document\" Hook", "text": "Start the chapter outside the immediate narrative using an artifact. Excellent for world-building without info-dumping.\n Examples: A snippet from a military tribunal transcript, an excerpt from an in-universe historical text, a captain’s log, a diagnostic report on a failing piece of machinery, or a fragmented radio transmission from an Okinawa quarantine zone.", "example": "Recovered voicemail, 03:12: If this reaches you, leave the spare key where we agreed.", "description": "Start the chapter outside the immediate narrative using an artifact. Excellent for world-building without info-dumping."}, {"id": 2, "name": "The In Media Res Action Drop", "text": "Drop the reader directly into the middle of an action sequence with no preamble or context. Let them catch up as the scene unfolds.\n Execution: Begin mid-swing, mid-shout, or mid-retreat. Avoid explaining why the characters are running until paragraph three or four.", "example": "Mara caught the railing with one hand. Below her, the ferry began to move.", "description": "Drop the reader directly into the middle of an action sequence with no preamble or context. Let them catch up as the scene unfolds."}, {"id": 3, "name": "The Micro-Sensory Focus", "text": "Instead of describing the whole room or setting, start with extreme hyper-focus on a single, often uncomfortable, sensory detail. \n Execution: The smell of ozone, the rhythmic dripping of condensation on a metal grate, the grit of sand between teeth, or the specific pitch of a distant siren.", "example": "A grain of sand clicked between his back teeth every time he swallowed.", "description": "Instead of describing the whole room or setting, start with extreme hyper-focus on a single, often uncomfortable, sensory detail."}, {"id": 4, "name": "The Cinematic \"Wide-to-Narrow\" Pan", "text": "Establish the macro scale of the setting before zooming in on the micro reality of your point-of-view character.\n Execution: Describe the massive scale of a fleet in orbit, the sprawling geography of an island containment zone, or the architecture of a facility, then narrow the lens sharply down to the protagonist lacing up their boots in a cramped room.", "example": "From the ridge, the city looked intact. In the last lit apartment, a woman washed the same cup for the third time.", "description": "Establish the macro scale of the setting before zooming in on the micro reality of your point-of-view character."}, {"id": 5, "name": "The Thematic Thesis (The \"Victor Hugo\" Opening)", "text": "Open with a broad philosophical statement, an observation about human nature, or a reflection on the mechanics of the universe.\n Execution: State a universal \"truth\" about war, justice, or fear, and then immediately juxtapose it with the specific, messy reality your character is currently facing.", "example": "A promise becomes expensive when someone remembers it. Daniel had hoped his brother wouldn't.", "description": "Open with a broad philosophical statement, an observation about human nature, or a reflection on the mechanics of the universe."}, {"id": 6, "name": "The Disembodied Dialogue", "text": "Start with a line of dialogue—or an exchange of dialogue—before the reader knows who is speaking or where they are.\n Execution: Use a sharp, provocative, or confusing statement. \"Cut the hardline,\" or \"She’s not breathing.\" Follow the exchange for a few beats before grounding the reader in the setting.", "example": "“You brought the wrong child.” The voice came from behind the kitchen door.", "description": "Start with a line of dialogue—or an exchange of dialogue—before the reader knows who is speaking or where they are."}, {"id": 7, "name": "The Temporal Marker or \"Ticking Clock\"", "text": "Orient the reader purely through time, establishing immediate dread or urgency.\n Execution: \"It had been forty-two hours since the last transmission,\" or \"Sunrise was exactly three minutes away.\"", "example": "In eleven minutes, the tide would cover the path. He still hadn't found her shoes.", "description": "Orient the reader purely through time, establishing immediate dread or urgency."}, {"id": 8, "name": "The Subverted Routine", "text": "Begin by describing a character going through a mundane, highly practiced routine, and end the opening paragraph by breaking that routine entirely.\n Execution: The familiar process of breaking down a rifle, preparing legal briefs, or running a perimeter check, violently interrupted by the inciting incident of the chapter.", "example": "She counted the till, turned off the sign, and checked the back door. Tonight, someone checked it from the other side.", "description": "Begin by describing a character going through a mundane, highly practiced routine, and end the opening paragraph by breaking that routine entirely."}, {"id": 9, "name": "The Ominous Fact / Dramatic Irony", "text": "State a cold, hard fact that the reader knows, the narrator knows, but the characters in the scene do not know.\n Execution: \"No one in the squad knew that the floor beneath them was rigged to blow.\"", "example": "The driver didn't know that his passenger had built the bridge they were about to cross.", "description": "State a cold, hard fact that the reader knows, the narrator knows, but the characters in the scene do not know."}, {"id": 10, "name": "The Memory or Dream Transition", "text": "Open with a surreal or fragmented memory that seamlessly—or jarringly—bleeds into the present reality.\n Execution: A memory of a peaceful moment or a past trauma that is interrupted by a character being shaken awake or snapping back to attention in a hostile environment.", "example": "In the dream, his mother was still teaching him to float. He woke with someone counting compressions against his chest.", "description": "Open with a surreal or fragmented memory that seamlessly—or jarringly—bleeds into the present reality."}, {"id": 11, "name": "The Objective Status Report", "text": "Strip away all emotion and describe the state of the protagonist or the squad with cold, clinical detachment.\n Execution: List the remaining ammunition, the physical injuries, the temperature drops, or the structural integrity of their shelter as an itemized inventory of desperation.", "example": "Water: two bottles. Working radios: none. People willing to admit they were lost: one.", "description": "Strip away all emotion and describe the state of the protagonist or the squad with cold, clinical detachment."}, {"id": 12, "name": "The Flash-Forward Setup", "text": "Open with the chaotic climax of the chapter, then immediately hit the reader with \"Four hours earlier,\" forcing them to read just to figure out how things went so wrong.", "example": "By sunset, the wedding guests would be searching the river. At noon, the bride was still choosing earrings.", "description": "Open with the chaotic climax of the chapter, then immediately hit the reader with \"Four hours earlier,\" forcing them to read just to figure out how things went so wrong."}, {"id": 13, "name": "The Retrospective Regret", "text": "The narrator speaks from a point in the future, framing the upcoming scene with dread. (Example: \"If she had known that was the last time she’d see the sun, she wouldn't have spent it compiling code.\")", "example": "I wish I'd let the telephone ring. Instead, I answered with a joke.", "description": "The narrator speaks from a point in the future, framing the upcoming scene with dread."}, {"id": 14, "name": "The \"Negative Space\" Opening", "text": "Describe exactly what is missing from a scene to establish the tone. (Example: \"There was no static on the comms, no ambient hum from the servers, and worse, no screaming.\")", "example": "The bell above the shop door was gone. Mara pushed the door again, watching the bare hook.", "description": "Describe exactly what is missing from a scene to establish the tone."}, {"id": 15, "name": "The \"How We Got Here\" Rapid Recap", "text": "A cynical or breathless one-paragraph summary of the disastrous decisions that led to the current moment.", "example": "We'd missed the boat, borrowed a car, and trusted a man who introduced himself with a different name at every stop. That was how we reached the lighthouse.", "description": "A cynical or breathless one-paragraph summary of the disastrous decisions that led to the current moment."}, {"id": 16, "name": "The Lingering Hangover", "text": "Start entirely focused on the physical or emotional wreckage left by the previous chapter before moving the plot forward.\n---", "example": "The argument had ended an hour ago. She was still holding the cracked plate.", "description": "Start entirely focused on the physical or emotional wreckage left by the previous chapter before moving the plot forward."}, {"id": 17, "name": "The Immediate Dilemma", "text": "Open with a harsh, binary choice the protagonist is actively weighing. (Example: \"Cut the oxygen to the lower decks, or let the cereus parasite reach the bridge.\")", "example": "He could give the letter to his sister or burn it before she woke. The kettle began to whistle.", "description": "Open with a harsh, binary choice the protagonist is actively weighing."}, {"id": 18, "name": "The Deceptive Calm", "text": "Paint a picture of perfect, pristine safety that is so idyllic the reader immediately knows something terrible is about to happen.", "example": "Every towel was folded. Every flower faced the window. Even the dog appeared to have been posed.", "description": "Paint a picture of perfect, pristine safety that is so idyllic the reader immediately knows something terrible is about to happen."}, {"id": 19, "name": "The Obsessive Tic", "text": "Focus entirely on a character’s physical coping mechanism—picking at a thumbnail, disassembling and reassembling a mag, tapping a rhythm on a console—to show, not tell, their anxiety.", "example": "She kept smoothing the receipt against her knee. The ink had already rubbed away.", "description": "Focus entirely on a character’s physical coping mechanism—picking at a thumbnail, disassembling and reassembling a mag, tapping a rhythm on a console—to show, not tell, their anxiety."}, {"id": 20, "name": "The Unanswered Question", "text": "Start the chapter with an internal or spoken question that frames the entire thematic conflict of the scene.", "example": "How long could you keep someone's room before it became a place you were hiding?", "description": "Start the chapter with an internal or spoken question that frames the entire thematic conflict of the scene."}, {"id": 21, "name": "The List", "text": "A rapid-fire, stream-of-consciousness catalog of a character's racing thoughts, priorities, or physical injuries.", "example": "Passport. Medicine. The photograph he couldn't look at. Everything else could stay.", "description": "A rapid-fire, stream-of-consciousness catalog of a character's racing thoughts, priorities, or physical injuries."}, {"id": 22, "name": "The Shift in Perspective", "text": "Open the chapter from the point of view of an NPC, an enemy combatant, or even the hostile environment itself watching the protagonist approach.\n---", "example": "From beneath the pier, the creature watched the fisherman bait his hook.", "description": "Open the chapter from the point of view of an NPC, an enemy combatant, or even the hostile environment itself watching the protagonist approach."}, {"id": 23, "name": "The Biological Extreme Close-Up", "text": "Bypass the surroundings entirely and start with visceral, uncomfortable bodily realities. (Example: \"Lactic acid burned like battery fluid in his calves, and his pulse hammered a desperate warning against his eardrums.\")", "example": "His tongue had swollen against the broken tooth. Each breath tasted of copper.", "description": "Bypass the surroundings entirely and start with visceral, uncomfortable bodily realities."}, {"id": 24, "name": "The Weather/Atmosphere Override", "text": "Make the environment the primary antagonist of the first paragraph. Acid rain, suffocating humidity, or a vacuum drop that demands immediate attention.", "example": "Rain filled the footprints before they could decide which set to follow.", "description": "Make the environment the primary antagonist of the first paragraph. Acid rain, suffocating humidity, or a vacuum drop that demands immediate attention."}, {"id": 25, "name": "The Auditory Assault", "text": "Describe a sudden, deafening noise—or a highly specific, terrifying sound—that shatters a quiet moment.", "example": "The sound came again: a coin dropped onto glass, somewhere inside the sealed wall.", "description": "Describe a sudden, deafening noise—or a highly specific, terrifying sound—that shatters a quiet moment."}, {"id": 26, "name": "The Topographical Orientation", "text": "Orient the reader strictly by geography and geometry, emphasizing isolation or tactical disadvantage. (Example: \"They were four hundred meters below sea level, backed against a limestone ridge.\")\n---", "example": "The road lay thirty meters above them. Between the road and the beach, the cliff offered no ledge wider than a hand.", "description": "Orient the reader strictly by geography and geometry, emphasizing isolation or tactical disadvantage."}, {"id": 27, "name": "The Bureaucratic Preamble", "text": "Open with the dry, impenetrable text of a legal statute, corporate policy, or treaty that the characters are about to violently violate, setting the stage for a tense courtroom or tribunal.", "example": "Residents must report all unregistered guests before dawn. Under the notice, someone had written: Even family?", "description": "Open with the dry, impenetrable text of a legal statute, corporate policy, or treaty that the characters are about to violently violate, setting the stage for a tense courtroom or tribunal."}, {"id": 28, "name": "The Lore/Mythology Drop", "text": "Briefly recount an ancient myth, historical battle, or local legend that perfectly mirrors the current predicament.", "example": "The village told a story about a well that returned whatever you regretted. Until that morning, Lena had considered it a warning about litter.", "description": "Briefly recount an ancient myth, historical battle, or local legend that perfectly mirrors the current predicament."}, {"id": 29, "name": "The Paradox", "text": "Start with a deliberately contradictory statement that forces the reader to pause and recalibrate. (Example: \"The only thing more dangerous than a loaded weapon was an empty courtroom.\")", "example": "The safest room in the house was the one nobody could leave.", "description": "Start with a deliberately contradictory statement that forces the reader to pause and recalibrate."}, {"id": 30, "name": "The Institutional Critique", "text": "The narrator offers a cynical observation about the military, the legal system, or the government that grounds the scene in systemic frustration.", "example": "The department could lose a person in an afternoon and require six weeks to admit it had misplaced a chair.", "description": "The narrator offers a cynical observation about the military, the legal system, or the government that grounds the scene in systemic frustration."}, {"id": 31, "name": "The Technical Diagnostic", "text": "Open with pure, unadulterated jargon or a system output that implies disaster to anyone who knows how to read it. (Example: \"Core temp: 180°C. Containment field: 10%. Override protocol: offline.\")\n6. Classical & Epic (Greco-Roman)", "example": "DOOR SEAL: intact. OCCUPANTS: 4. REGISTERED CAPACITY: 3.", "description": "Open with pure, unadulterated jargon or a system output that implies disaster to anyone who knows how to read it."}, {"id": 32, "name": "The Invocation of the Muse", "text": "Open by formally asking a god, muse, or higher power to grant the telling — which doubles as a thesis statement of the work's subject.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Iliad begins by calling on the goddess to sing the wrath of Achilles; the Aeneid announces \"arms and the man.\"\nSpin: Open a chapter as a declassified preamble that \"invokes\" its authorizing body — a request to the archive, the tribunal, or the AI itself to render what follows.", "example": "Archive, lend me the names you kept when the families couldn't.", "description": "Open by formally asking a god, muse, or higher power to grant the telling — which doubles as a thesis statement of the work's subject."}, {"id": 33, "name": "The Programmatic Proem", "text": "The narrator states up front what this account is, why it matters, and how it was gathered — establishing authority before any events.\n\n\nTouchstone: Herodotus opens by presenting his inquiry; Thucydides opens by arguing his war is the greatest yet recorded.\nSpin: A field historian or after-action analyst frames the chapter by claiming its significance before showing it.", "example": "This account concerns the six minutes omitted from the official record. I reconstructed them from a receipt and two incompatible testimonies.", "description": "The narrator states up front what this account is, why it matters, and how it was gathered — establishing authority before any events."}, {"id": 34, "name": "The Epic Catalogue", "text": "Open with a sweeping enumeration — forces, ships, names, holdings — where the sheer scale is the effect.\n\n\nTouchstone: Homer's Catalogue of Ships.\nSpin: A muster roll, a manifest, or a casualty ledger read as grim poetry.\n\n\n\n7. Sacred & Scriptural Openings", "example": "Four boats. Seventeen lanterns. Ninety names read from a list that had room for twenty.", "description": "Open with a sweeping enumeration — forces, ships, names, holdings — where the sheer scale is the effect."}, {"id": 35, "name": "The Cosmogonic \"In the Beginning\"", "text": "Start at an origin point — of a world, an order, a conflict — in a register of creation.\n\n\nTouchstone: Genesis and the Gospel of John both open at the literal beginning of things.\nSpin: The origin of the parasite, the facility, or the war, narrated as a creation myth the in-universe culture now repeats.", "example": "In the beginning, the town had one road and no reason to close it.", "description": "Start at an origin point — of a world, an order, a conflict — in a register of creation."}, {"id": 36, "name": "The Naming of the Power (Invocation / Basmala)", "text": "Begin by naming the authority under whose sign the text proceeds.\n\n\nTouchstone: Many Arabic and Persian works open by naming God; Ferdowsi's Shahnameh opens in the name of the Lord of soul and wisdom.\nSpin: A chapter that opens under the seal and authority of an agency — \"By order of…\" — before a word of narrative.", "example": "By the authority of the Night Office, this testimony may now be heard.", "description": "Begin by naming the authority under whose sign the text proceeds."}, {"id": 37, "name": "The Prophetic Call / Vision Commission", "text": "Open with a figure being summoned — given a vision, a word, or a burden they did not ask for.\n\n\nTouchstone: The prophetic books open with the word coming to someone.\nSpin: A soldier or analyst receiving an order, a transmission, or a hallucination that conscripts them into the chapter's events.", "example": "The message arrived in her own handwriting. It instructed her to be at the river before she was born.", "description": "Open with a figure being summoned — given a vision, a word, or a burden they did not ask for."}, {"id": 38, "name": "The Parable Frame", "text": "Begin obliquely with a small illustrative story whose meaning the chapter will detonate.\n\n\nTouchstone: \"A certain man went down…\" — the parable's deliberately generic opening.\nSpin: A barracks legend or a piece of in-universe folklore told straight, then weaponized by events.\n\n\n\n8. Frame-Tale & Oral-Transmission Markers (cross-cultural)", "example": "A woman once taught a locked door to recognize her voice. When she grew old, she discovered the problem with that kindness.", "description": "Begin obliquely with a small illustrative story whose meaning the chapter will detonate."}, {"id": 39, "name": "The Frame Return", "text": "Don't open on the action — open on the outer story resuming, the teller picking the thread back up.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Thousand and One Nights returns each night to Scheherazade; the Canterbury Tales and Decameron return to their travelers.\nSpin: Return to the interrogation room, the debrief, or Booker-style narrator between embedded accounts.", "example": "The investigator replaced the tape. “Start again with the man at the window.”", "description": "Don't open on the action — open on the outer story resuming, the teller picking the thread back up."}, {"id": 40, "name": "The Transmission Tag", "text": "Open with the oral marker that flags this as received knowledge, passed hand to hand.\n\n\nTouchstone: Chinese vernacular novels open with huàshuō (\"the story goes that…\"); countless traditions open \"It is related that…\" / \"They say…\".\nSpin: \"The record states…\" / \"Per the recovered log…\" — your found-document aesthetic as a recurring incantation.", "example": "They say the keeper stopped winding the clock after the last ferry. The maintenance ledger gives a different date.", "description": "Open with the oral marker that flags this as received knowledge, passed hand to hand."}, {"id": 41, "name": "The Chapter-Argument Couplet", "text": "Open every chapter with a short verse or paired-line summary of what it contains — a built-in headline.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Chinese zhanghui novel (e.g., Romance of the Three Kingdoms, Water Margin) heads each hui with a balanced couplet.\nSpin: A two-line classified subject-header per chapter, redaction-bar included — a DOMARE table of contents that's also poetry.", "example": "A daughter returns with a borrowed name. A father opens an empty room.", "description": "Open every chapter with a short verse or paired-line summary of what it contains — a built-in headline."}, {"id": 42, "name": "The Call to Attention", "text": "Open by demanding the audience listen — a verbal seizing of the room.\n\n\nTouchstone: Beowulf opens with \"Hwæt!\"; medieval minstrels opened with \"Oyez\"; West African griots open by asking leave to tell.\nSpin: A broadcast cold-open — \"All stations, all stations\" — or an instructor silencing a briefing room.\n\n\n\n9. East Asian Traditions", "example": "Listen before you open the hatch. Whatever you hear after that will sound like permission.", "description": "Open by demanding the audience listen — a verbal seizing of the room."}, {"id": 43, "name": "The \"Long Ago Now\" Tale-Opener", "text": "A fixed formula that drops the listener into mythic past-time instantly.\n\n\nTouchstone: Japanese setsuwa open Ima wa mukashi (\"now it is long ago\"); folktales open mukashi mukashi.\nSpin: \"Before the quarantine, there was a city…\" — a fairy-tale cadence applied to recent, traumatic history.", "example": "Long before the town became a warning on a map, it was a place you went for peaches.", "description": "A fixed formula that drops the listener into mythic past-time instantly."}, {"id": 44, "name": "The Seasonal Threshold", "text": "Open not on plot but on the season — a single seasonal image that sets the entire emotional weather.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Pillow Book opens \"In spring, the dawn\"; the renga/haiku tradition anchors openings to a season-word (kigo).\nSpin: Open on Okinawa's humidity, typhoon tension, or the specific light of a season before any character speaks.", "example": "The first fallen persimmon split beside the gate. Summer's visitors had finally gone.", "description": "Open not on plot but on the season — a single seasonal image that sets the entire emotional weather."}, {"id": 45, "name": "The Impermanence Bell (mono no aware)", "text": "Open on transience itself — a sound or image that announces that all of this is already passing.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Tale of the Heike opens on the Gion temple bell echoing the impermanence of all things.\nSpin: The cooling tick of a dead reactor, a last functioning beacon — sound as a memento mori for the chapter.", "example": "The cooling engine ticked beneath the abandoned bus. Each sound seemed smaller than the one before it.", "description": "Open on transience itself — a sound or image that announces that all of this is already passing."}, {"id": 46, "name": "The Following-the-Brush Drift (zuihitsu)", "text": "Open in apparent idleness, one associative thought leading to the next, until the drift lands somewhere sharp.\n\n\nTouchstone: Kenkō's Tsurezuregusa opens \"in idleness\"; Sei Shōnagon's lists wander by association.\nSpin: A journaling voice (your Prima Materia register) meandering through small observations before the chapter's blade drops.", "example": "I've always disliked blue mugs. Perhaps because of that kitchen, though the walls were yellow, and the woman who lived there drank from a jar.", "description": "Open in apparent idleness, one associative thought leading to the next, until the drift lands somewhere sharp."}, {"id": 47, "name": "The Oblique Court Introduction", "text": "Introduce a central figure indirectly — by rank, rumor, or circumstance — without naming them.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Tale of Genji opens \"in a certain reign there was a lady…\".\nSpin: \"There was an officer whose name the files no longer carry…\" — introduction by redaction.", "example": "There was a clerk whose signature appeared on every missing file. Nobody could agree where her desk had been.", "description": "Introduce a central figure indirectly — by rank, rumor, or circumstance — without naming them."}, {"id": 48, "name": "The Cosmological-Historical Frame", "text": "Open with a vast cyclical law of history, then drop into the human-scale instance of it.\n\n\nTouchstone: Three Kingdoms opens on the maxim that the empire long divided must unite, long united must divide.\nSpin: A line on the eternal cycle of containment and breach, then one soldier at one airlock.", "example": "Every wall eventually becomes a boundary between two versions of the same story. This one ran through a man's kitchen.", "description": "Open with a vast cyclical law of history, then drop into the human-scale instance of it."}, {"id": 49, "name": "The Kishōtenketsu Pivot", "text": "Structure the opening around a coming turn rather than a conflict — introduction, development, then an oblique twist that recontextualizes, then reconciliation. Tension comes from juxtaposition, not collision.\n\n\nTouchstone: The four-act ki-shō-ten-ketsu logic underlying much East Asian narrative.\nSpin: Open two unrelated threads calmly; let the \"ten\" be the reader's realization that they're the same event seen twice.\n\n\n\n10. Persian, Arabic & Islamicate", "example": "The old man fed pigeons every morning. Across the square, a girl counted them. When one failed to return, both left their homes carrying the same photograph.", "description": "Structure the opening around a coming turn rather than a conflict — introduction, development, then an oblique twist that recontextualizes, then reconciliation. Tension comes from juxtaposition, not collision."}, {"id": 50, "name": "The Praise-Prelude", "text": "Open with a passage of praise or reflection before the narrative proper — a tuning of the instrument.\n\n\nTouchstone: Persian masnavi (Rumi, Ferdowsi) traditionally open with praise of the divine and of intellect.\nSpin: An epigraph venerating the institution, the mission, or the machine — sincere on its face, ironic in context.", "example": "Praise the lamp that kept the harbor visible. Praise the hand that remembered to replace its oil.", "description": "Open with a passage of praise or reflection before the narrative proper — a tuning of the instrument."}, {"id": 51, "name": "The Object That Speaks Its Grief", "text": "Open in the voice of a thing, whose lament becomes the chapter's theme.\n\n\nTouchstone: Rumi's Masnavi opens with the reed-flute lamenting its separation from the reed bed.\nSpin: A salvaged black box, a dying AI, or a weapon \"narrating\" its own use and longing.", "example": "I was made to carry water. They filled me with ashes and called me precious.", "description": "Open in the voice of a thing, whose lament becomes the chapter's theme."}, {"id": 52, "name": "\"Once There Was, Once There Was Not\"", "text": "Open with a paradox that suspends the tale between truth and fiction.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Persian folk-opener yeki bud yeki nabud.\nSpin: \"It happened, and the record denies it happened\" — perfect for a story about classification and deniability.", "example": "There was once a house at the end of this road. There has never been a road here.", "description": "Open with a paradox that suspends the tale between truth and fiction."}, {"id": 53, "name": "The Maqāma Encounter", "text": "Open with a wandering narrator stumbling onto an eloquent stranger/trickster whose performance is the chapter.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Arabic maqāmāt of al-Harīrī — rhymed prose, recurring rogue.\nSpin: A recurring informant or con artist the protagonist keeps re-encountering in new disguises.\n\n\n\n11. South Asian", "example": "At the checkpoint, a stranger offered to sell me my own name. He pronounced it better than I did.", "description": "Open with a wandering narrator stumbling onto an eloquent stranger/trickster whose performance is the chapter."}, {"id": 54, "name": "The Benediction Verse (maṅgalācaraṇa)", "text": "Open with a short auspicious invocation that blesses the undertaking of the text.\n\n\nTouchstone: The conventional invocatory verse opening classical Sanskrit works.\nSpin: A one-line \"may this record survive its readers\" charm heading the chapter.", "example": "May the hand that opens this ledger have less to forgive than the hand that closed it.", "description": "Open with a short auspicious invocation that blesses the undertaking of the text."}, {"id": 55, "name": "The Nested Didactic Tale", "text": "Open a story inside a story inside a lesson — frames within frames, each teaching.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Panchatantra / Hitopadesha embed fables several layers deep.\nSpin: A mentor tells a cadet a story; inside it, a character tells another story that's the real warning.", "example": "My teacher told me about a captain who learned courage from a cook. The cook, he said, had learned it from a mouse.", "description": "Open a story inside a story inside a lesson — frames within frames, each teaching."}, {"id": 56, "name": "The Mythic-Time Placement", "text": "Locate the chapter in a fixed legendary past with a formulaic stamp.\n\n\nTouchstone: Jātaka tales open \"long ago, when Brahmadatta reigned in Benares…\".\nSpin: \"In the years the Affairs Office still answered its phones…\" — a stamp that dates the chapter to a lost era.\n\n\n\n12. Oral Epic & Indigenous Traditions", "example": "In the years when the Office still answered its own telephone, a woman called to report a missing moon.", "description": "Locate the chapter in a fixed legendary past with a formulaic stamp."}, {"id": 57, "name": "The Teller's Self-Authentication", "text": "The narrator opens by naming themselves and their right to tell this — the credential is the opening.\n\n\nTouchstone: In the Sundiata epic, the griot (jeli) opens by declaring who he is and that he is the keeper of the word.\nSpin: A narrator who opens by establishing their clearance and chain of custody for the account.", "example": "My name is Sela. I kept the ferry register for thirty years, and I crossed out only one passenger.", "description": "The narrator opens by naming themselves and their right to tell this — the credential is the opening."}, {"id": 58, "name": "The Saga Deadpan", "text": "Open with flat, unadorned fact: a man, where he lived, who his father was. No drama — the drama comes later, and the flatness makes it land harder.\n\n\nTouchstone: Icelandic family sagas (Njáls saga, Egils saga) open with terse genealogy and geography.\nSpin: \"There was a sergeant named ——. He was posted to the ridge. His mother had served before him.\" Cold, then catastrophic.", "example": "A man named Tomas lived beside the quarry. His father had lived there before him. Neither had worked the stone.", "description": "Open with flat, unadorned fact: a man, where he lived, who his father was. No drama — the drama comes later, and the flatness makes it land harder."}, {"id": 59, "name": "The Singer's Desire", "text": "Open by stating the urge to tell — the telling framed as something welling up and demanding release.\n\n\nTouchstone: The Finnish Kalevala opens with the singer declaring a mind/desire to sing.\nSpin: A character compelled to record testimony they were ordered to forget.", "example": "I've carried this account longer than I carried the man it concerns. Today I'm setting it down.", "description": "Open by stating the urge to tell — the telling framed as something welling up and demanding release."}, {"id": 60, "name": "Seasonal / Ceremonial Gating", "text": "The opening signals that a story may only be told under certain conditions — and the telling itself is therefore a transgression or a rite.\n\n\nTouchstone: In several Indigenous North American traditions, including Diné (Navajo) practice, certain stories are properly told only in winter; the time of telling is part of the story's meaning. (Worth handling with care and research rather than imitation — but the underlying device, \"this may only be spoken now, and that's why it matters,\" is powerful.)\nSpin: A log that \"may only be opened after the operation is dead\" — the reader breaking a seal by reading.", "example": "The envelope could be opened only after both signatories had died. This morning, the second one knocked on my door.", "description": "The opening signals that a story may only be told under certain conditions — and the telling itself is therefore a transgression or a rite."}, {"id": 61, "name": "Mythic-Time Entry", "text": "Open in an undifferentiated originary time before the present order.\n\n\nTouchstone: Australian Aboriginal storytelling opens in the Dreaming; many traditions open \"when the animals were still people.\"\nSpin: Before the cordon, before the maps were reclassified — a chapter that opens in the world's prior shape.\n\n\n\n13. Medieval & Renaissance European", "example": "Before the island had a name, the people who lived there used the shape of its shadow.", "description": "Open in an undifferentiated originary time before the present order."}, {"id": 62, "name": "The Dream-Vision Threshold", "text": "The narrator falls asleep (or into reverie) and the chapter unfolds as an allegorical dream.\n\n\nTouchstone: Dante opens the Commedia midway through life's journey, lost; Piers Plowman and Pearl open into dreams.\nSpin: A drugged, concussed, or cryo-thawed POV whose chapter blurs vision and reality — and you decide later which it was.", "example": "Sleep found him at the desk. In the dream, every drawer opened onto the same field.", "description": "The narrator falls asleep (or into reverie) and the chapter unfolds as an allegorical dream."}, {"id": 63, "name": "The Dedicatory Epistle", "text": "Open with an address to a patron or reader about the work, before the work begins.\n\n\nTouchstone: Renaissance works routinely open with a letter to a patron.\nSpin: A transmittal memo (\"Per your request, find enclosed…\") heading a chapter of recovered material.", "example": "To whoever inherits this house: the locked room is smaller than the arguments about it.", "description": "Open with an address to a patron or reader about the work, before the work begins."}, {"id": 64, "name": "The Estates Catalogue", "text": "Open by parading a gallery of character-types one after another, sketching a whole social world.\n\n\nTouchstone: The General Prologue of the Canterbury Tales introduces its pilgrims in turn.\nSpin: A roll-call of a squad or a station's personnel, each thumbnail loaded with foreshadowing.", "example": "The mayor arrived with flowers, the undertaker with an umbrella, and the schoolteacher with an empty chair.", "description": "Open by parading a gallery of character-types one after another, sketching a whole social world."}, {"id": 65, "name": "The Pseudo-Translation Frame", "text": "Open by insisting the text is merely a translation or transcription of a real document the author found.\n\n\nTouchstone: Don Quixote attributes itself to a found Arabic manuscript; The Castle of Otranto posed as a translated medieval chronicle.\nSpin: \"Translated from the recovered field journal; bracketed text reconstructed.\" The conceit you already love, with the translator's own voice intruding.\n\n\n\n14. The Novel, 18th–19th Century", "example": "Translator's note: The witness uses the same word for shelter and surrender. I've preserved the ambiguity.", "description": "Open by insisting the text is merely a translation or transcription of a real document the author found."}, {"id": 66, "name": "The Aphoristic Social Truth", "text": "Open with a confident universal maxim — which the chapter then proceeds to complicate or demolish.\n\n\nTouchstone: Austen opens Pride and Prejudice on a \"truth universally acknowledged\"; Tolstoy opens Anna Karenina on happy versus unhappy families.\nSpin: \"Every containment failure is alike; every cover-up fails in its own way.\"", "example": "Every respectable family keeps one story ready for visitors. Ours concerned a storm that had never reached our town.", "description": "Open with a confident universal maxim — which the chapter then proceeds to complicate or demolish."}, {"id": 67, "name": "The Essayistic Preamble", "text": "Open the chapter with the narrator's standalone essay or digression, only loosely tethered to the plot.\n\n\nTouchstone: Fielding opens each book of Tom Jones with a discursive chapter.\nSpin: A short treatise on bureaucracy, fear, or signal latency before the chapter's action — your Institutional Critique (#30) at full length.", "example": "There are several ways to wait for a letter. The most laborious is to pretend you've stopped expecting it.", "description": "Open the chapter with the narrator's standalone essay or digression, only loosely tethered to the plot."}, {"id": 68, "name": "The Direct Self-Naming / Address", "text": "Open by speaking straight to the reader, often by naming the narrator.\n\n\nTouchstone: Moby-Dick opens by inviting the reader to call him Ishmael.\nSpin: A testimony that opens \"You can call me whatever the file calls me\" — identity as something assigned, not owned.", "example": "Call me the name at the bottom of the form. It's the only one they've agreed to return.", "description": "Open by speaking straight to the reader, often by naming the narrator."}, {"id": 69, "name": "The Panoramic Omniscient Establishing Shot", "text": "Open with a god's-eye sweep over a whole city or era before descending — but held longer, more essayistic, more atmospheric.\n\n\nTouchstone: Bleak House opens on London fog; A Tale of Two Cities on the age of extremes.\nSpin: A long aerial pass over the quarantined island as a living organism before you find your one soldier in it.", "example": "Fog covered the harbor, softened the warehouses, and erased the road behind the bakery. In its kitchen, a boy watched his father pack a bag.", "description": "Open with a god's-eye sweep over a whole city or era before descending — but held longer, more essayistic, more atmospheric."}, {"id": 70, "name": "The Autobiographical Birth", "text": "Open a life-story at its literal start, with the narrator framing whether they'll even prove to be the hero.\n\n\nTouchstone: David Copperfield opens by questioning whether the narrator is the hero of his own life.\nSpin: A character's after-action statement that opens by doubting they were ever the protagonist of the operation.\n\n\n\n15. Modernist & Contemporary", "example": "I was born during an evacuation. My mother always said that explained my habit of leaving before the trouble started.", "description": "Open a life-story at its literal start, with the narrator framing whether they'll even prove to be the hero."}, {"id": 71, "name": "The Mid-Thought Consciousness", "text": "Drop into the unpunctuated flow of a mind already in motion — no scene-setting, just interiority.\n\n\nTouchstone: Woolf opens Mrs Dalloway mid-errand, in Clarissa's thought.\nSpin: Open inside a pilot's task-saturated headspace, the prose itself fragmenting under G-load.", "example": "Three cups, unless he brought her, then four, and where had she put the unchipped one?", "description": "Drop into the unpunctuated flow of a mind already in motion — no scene-setting, just interiority."}, {"id": 72, "name": "The Confessional Frame", "text": "Open with an admission that colors everything after — the narrator confessing the difficulty or shame of telling.\n\n\nTouchstone: Ford Madox Ford opens The Good Soldier by calling it the saddest story he's heard.\nSpin: A debrief that opens \"I've told this wrong every time I've told it\" — an unreliable-witness flag raised on line one.", "example": "I've told this as a rescue for so long that I scarcely remember who first asked to leave.", "description": "Open with an admission that colors everything after — the narrator confessing the difficulty or shame of telling."}, {"id": 73, "name": "The Second-Person Address", "text": "Make the reader the protagonist; \"you\" do the chapter's actions.\n\n\nTouchstone: Calvino's If on a winter's night a traveler addresses the reader as the one acting.\nSpin: A training-simulation or interrogation chapter narrated as \"You enter the room. You are not supposed to be here.\"", "example": "You recognize the room before you recognize the person waiting in it.", "description": "Make the reader the protagonist; \"you\" do the chapter's actions."}, {"id": 74, "name": "The Retrospective-Prospective Fold", "text": "Open from a future vantage looking back, compressing decades into one sentence that pivots from doom to a tender memory.\n\n\nTouchstone: García Márquez opens One Hundred Years of Solitude with a man before a firing squad recalling the distant day he first saw ice. (Paraphrased — the line is in copyright.)\nSpin: \"Years after the tribunal, what she'd remember was not the verdict but the smell of the courtroom's air conditioning.\" Fuses your #12 (Flash-Forward) and #13 (Retrospective Regret) into one motion.", "example": "Years after the hearing, she would remember the witness by the way he'd saved half his orange for the child beside him.", "description": "Open from a future vantage looking back, compressing decades into one sentence that pivots from doom to a tender memory."}, {"id": 75, "name": "The Matter-of-Fact Marvelous", "text": "State the impossible in the flat register of the ordinary — no astonishment, which makes it more unsettling.\n\n\nTouchstone: Magical realism's signature deadpan toward the supernatural.\nSpin: \"The parasite had been speaking for three days. Nobody on the night shift found this remarkable anymore.\"", "example": "Her husband returned from the dead on a Tuesday, which complicated the grocery order.", "description": "State the impossible in the flat register of the ordinary — no astonishment, which makes it more unsettling."}, {"id": 76, "name": "The Metafictional Aside", "text": "Open by acknowledging the artifact — that this is a record, a book, a constructed account — and letting that destabilize the reader.\n\n\nTouchstone: The self-aware narrator who reminds you you're reading.\nSpin: A chapter that opens by noting which pages of itself are missing, or that it was assembled by an AI from partial logs (Booker's voice).\n\n\n\n16. Genre Signatures", "example": "The next page is missing. What remains of its corner suggests someone tried to burn it twice.", "description": "Open by acknowledging the artifact — that this is a record, a book, a constructed account — and letting that destabilize the reader."}, {"id": 77, "name": "The Recurring Invented Epigraph (systematized #1)", "text": "Head every chapter with a quotation from a fictional in-world source, building a second, ghostly text alongside the novel.\n\n\nTouchstone: Dune heads chapters with the writings of Princess Irulan; Foundation uses Encyclopedia Galactica entries.\nSpin: Each chapter opens with a DOMARE memo, a redacted field manual line, or a Booker annotation — your aesthetic's natural home.", "example": "“A closed gate is also an invitation to study the wall.” — Handbook for Visiting Inspectors, third edition", "description": "Head every chapter with a quotation from a fictional in-world source, building a second, ghostly text alongside the novel."}, {"id": 78, "name": "The Noir Weather-and-Mood", "text": "Open on weather and atmosphere doing the emotional work, voice dripping with weary attitude.\n\n\nTouchstone: The hardboiled detective opening — climate as character, cynicism as register.\nSpin: Okinawan rain on a tin roof, a narrator too tired to be afraid yet.", "example": "Rain worked its way through my collar with the persistence of a former employer. The address on the card was already running.", "description": "Open on weather and atmosphere doing the emotional work, voice dripping with weary attitude."}, {"id": 79, "name": "The Western Landscape-as-Fate", "text": "Open on a vast, indifferent landscape that dwarfs and predetermines the human figures crossing it.\n\n\nTouchstone: The frontier opening where the land is the real antagonist.\nSpin: A salt flat, a containment perimeter, an ocean trench — geography as verdict. (Overlaps your #26, Topographical Orientation, but at mythic scale.)", "example": "The plain offered a hundred miles of visibility and nowhere a man could disappear. He rode into it anyway.", "description": "Open on a vast, indifferent landscape that dwarfs and predetermines the human figures crossing it."}, {"id": 80, "name": "The Procedural Slug-Line", "text": "Open with a formatted operational header — time, grid, unit, status — that reads as authentic documentation.\n\n\nTouchstone: The military/thriller timestamp opening (\"0347 LOCAL — SECTOR 7 — EYES ONLY\").\nSpin: Your native dialect. Fuse with #31 (Technical Diagnostic) for a header that's already a disaster to the trained reader.", "example": "0517 LOCAL — EAST PIER — FINAL DEPARTURE LOG", "description": "Open with a formatted operational header — time, grid, unit, status — that reads as authentic documentation."}, {"id": 81, "name": "The Newsreel / Documentary Montage", "text": "Open with a collage of headlines, broadcast fragments, ad copy, and overheard speech — the era's noise before the individual story.\n\n\nTouchstone: Dos Passos's newsreel sections in U.S.A.\nSpin: A scroll of intercepted comms, propaganda banners, and panic-channel chatter before you cut to one quiet character inside the noise.", "example": "PORT REOPENS MONDAY. A radio promised clear skies. Outside the terminal, a handwritten sign asked whether anyone had seen a boy in a yellow coat.", "description": "Open with a collage of headlines, broadcast fragments, ad copy, and overheard speech — the era's noise before the individual story."}], "structures": [{"id": 1, "name": "Kishōtenketsu", "text": "[Japanese/Chinese verse] — four movements resolved by a context-shifting twist, not conflict.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "The turn must change how we read the earlier details. Conflict is optional in this exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Introduce a situation", "example": "A clerk sorts unclaimed letters."}, {"title": "Develop its pattern", "example": "Each letter has the same address."}, {"title": "Shift the context", "example": "The address belongs to a building demolished years ago."}, {"title": "Connect the parts", "example": "She realizes the letters chart the people displaced from it."}], "description": "Four movements: introduce, develop, shift the context, then connect the parts. Conflict can be present, but the contextual turn organizes this exercise.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 2, "name": "Sonata-Allegro", "text": "[Classical music] — two opposed themes stated, fractured in development, the antagonist-theme resolved \"home.\"", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "Treat themes as recurring values or images. This is a prose adaptation of a musical form.", "beats": [{"title": "State theme A", "example": "A pilot insists on following orders."}, {"title": "State theme B", "example": "A medic insists on rescuing strangers."}, {"title": "Develop both", "example": "Their separate decisions make each other's task harder."}, {"title": "Return in a changed key", "example": "The pilot turns rescue into the mission's governing order."}], "description": "two opposed themes stated, fractured in development, the antagonist-theme resolved \"home.\"", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 3, "name": "The Five Sandhis", "text": "[Sanskrit drama] — five junctures with a mandatory hesitation/doubt beat (vimarśa) before resolution.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "For this adapted exercise, make hesitation an actual decision point before the ending.", "beats": [{"title": "Plant the seed", "example": "A messenger accepts a sealed letter."}, {"title": "Advance the effort", "example": "She finds its intended recipient."}, {"title": "Make success possible", "example": "He agrees to open it."}, {"title": "Introduce doubt", "example": "The seal belongs to his executioner."}, {"title": "Complete the action", "example": "They read it together and choose to flee."}], "description": "five junctures with a mandatory hesitation/doubt beat (vimarśa) before resolution.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 4, "name": "Aristotelian Microcosm", "text": "[Greek] — the chapter as a complete miniature story ending at equilibrium.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "Resolve the chapter's local question while allowing the larger story to continue.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish the immediate need", "example": "A child wants her kite back."}, {"title": "Complicate the attempt", "example": "It hangs over a forbidden garden."}, {"title": "Create a decisive action", "example": "She asks the frightening owner for help."}, {"title": "Settle the local question", "example": "He returns it and opens the gate."}], "description": "the chapter as a complete miniature story ending at equilibrium.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 5, "name": "Scene–Sequel", "text": "[Swain] — Goal/Conflict/Disaster, then Reaction/Dilemma/Decision.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "Make the final decision create the next scene's goal. Disaster can be a modest setback.", "beats": [{"title": "Set a goal", "example": "A detective seeks a witness."}, {"title": "Block the attempt", "example": "The witness refuses."}, {"title": "Worsen the situation", "example": "The detective's visit exposes the hiding place."}, {"title": "Allow a reaction", "example": "She admits her mistake."}, {"title": "Weigh options", "example": "Hide together or run separately?"}, {"title": "Commit", "example": "She chooses to become the decoy."}], "description": "Goal/Conflict/Disaster, then Reaction/Dilemma/Decision.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 6, "name": "MICE Quotient", "text": "[Card] — resolve one thread-type (Milieu/Idea/Character/Event) before opening the next.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "This exercise uses nested MICE threads: close the inner question before the outer journey. The source's one-line summary is simplified.", "beats": [{"title": "Open a thread", "example": "A traveler enters a walled city."}, {"title": "Open a nested question", "example": "Why are the gates sealed?"}, {"title": "Answer the inner question", "example": "The gatekeeper is protecting a fugitive."}, {"title": "Close the outer thread", "example": "The traveler leaves with the fugitive."}], "description": "Organize nested threads by what they ask readers to wait for: a journey, an answer, a personal change, or a restored situation.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 7, "name": "In Media Res Sandwich", "text": "— open at the crisis, drop back for minimal context, blast past the opening.", "category": "Act & Conflict Spines", "rule": "Keep the backward step short. The return must advance beyond the opening moment.", "beats": [{"title": "Start at crisis", "example": "A diver cuts her air hose."}, {"title": "Step back briefly", "example": "Her partner has been dragged into a turbine."}, {"title": "Return to the crisis", "example": "The loose hose jams its blades."}, {"title": "Move beyond it", "example": "Both divers rise toward a surface they can't see."}], "description": "open at the crisis, drop back for minimal context, blast past the opening.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 8, "name": "Jo-ha-kyū", "text": "[Japanese; Hindustani raga variant] — slow → break → rapid accelerando; the raga variant binds the whole to one emotion and one hour.", "category": "Tempo & Acceleration", "rule": "Shorten the intervals between meaningful changes. This is a pacing exercise inspired by jo-ha-kyū.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin spaciously", "example": "A train approaches across the plain."}, {"title": "Break the pattern", "example": "Its signal never changes."}, {"title": "Accelerate", "example": "The driver brakes, passengers fall, the bridge opens."}], "description": "Shape the scene as a spacious beginning, a disruption, and a rapid finish.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 9, "name": "Shōnen Escalation", "text": "[manga] — power gap → analysis → beating → discover the hidden rule → reversal.", "category": "Tempo & Acceleration", "rule": "Seed the hidden rule before revealing it; the reversal should reward observation.", "beats": [{"title": "Expose the gap", "example": "A novice can't touch the champion."}, {"title": "Study the defeat", "example": "Every attack fails after the same bell."}, {"title": "Pay for another attempt", "example": "He loses his shield."}, {"title": "Discover the rule", "example": "The champion can only move while it rings."}, {"title": "Reverse the advantage", "example": "He catches the bell's clapper."}], "description": "power gap → analysis → beating → discover the hidden rule → reversal.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 10, "name": "Mecha Launch Sequence", "text": "[anime] — ritual synchronized buildup → massive kinetic release.", "category": "Tempo & Acceleration", "rule": "Give each preparation a distinct job. The buildup earns a clear release.", "beats": [{"title": "Prepare the ritual", "example": "The crew straps into their stations."}, {"title": "Synchronize tasks", "example": "Each operator confirms a different system."}, {"title": "Hold at a final barrier", "example": "One latch won't close."}, {"title": "Release the stored energy", "example": "The craft launches as the latch catches."}], "description": "ritual synchronized buildup → massive kinetic release.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 11, "name": "Ticking Clock / Real-Time", "text": "— chapter maps 1:1 to clock time; fragmented sentences burn the minutes.", "category": "Tempo & Acceleration", "rule": "Keep elapsed time plausible. Sentence fragments are optional; track the minutes through events.", "beats": [{"title": "Start the clock", "example": "The hearing resumes in five minutes."}, {"title": "Spend time visibly", "example": "A blocked corridor costs one minute."}, {"title": "Narrow the options", "example": "The elevator stalls."}, {"title": "Reach the deadline", "example": "The witness enters as the verdict begins."}], "description": "chapter maps 1:1 to clock time; fragmented sentences burn the minutes.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 12, "name": "Cathartic Release", "text": "— inverted tension: open at maximum, structure is one long exhale to safety.", "category": "Tempo & Acceleration", "rule": "Each beat should reduce immediate danger while revealing what surviving feels like.", "beats": [{"title": "Start at the peak", "example": "A survivor claws onto the rescue boat."}, {"title": "Remove one danger", "example": "A rope secures her."}, {"title": "Restore sensation", "example": "She feels a blanket and warm tea."}, {"title": "Allow release", "example": "She finally notices who's holding her hand."}], "description": "inverted tension: open at maximum, structure is one long exhale to safety.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 13, "name": "Theme & Variations", "text": "[music] — one scene-template replayed, each pass transformed.", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "Repeat a recognizable scene pattern, changing one meaningful variable each time.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish a template", "example": "A daughter calls home and asks about dinner."}, {"title": "Change the context", "example": "She calls from a distant city."}, {"title": "Change the power", "example": "Her father needs help preparing his meal."}, {"title": "Transform the meaning", "example": "She cooks while he stays on the line."}], "description": "one scene-template replayed, each pass transformed.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 14, "name": "Rondo (ABACA)", "text": "[music] — a refrain-scene returns between contrasting episodes, accruing menace.", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "Keep the returning scene recognizable. Each intervening episode changes what the return means.", "beats": [{"title": "A, establish a refrain", "example": "A guard checks an empty chair."}, {"title": "B, contrast", "example": "A prisoner describes the missing officer."}, {"title": "A, return", "example": "The chair is still empty."}, {"title": "C, contrast again", "example": "A radio broadcasts the officer's voice."}, {"title": "A, transform the refrain", "example": "Someone has warmed the chair."}], "description": "a refrain-scene returns between contrasting episodes, accruing menace.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 15, "name": "Colotomic Cycle", "text": "[Javanese gamelan] — stratified loops of different lengths all coinciding on a recurring \"gong.\"", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "Choose two or three repeat intervals and make their shared endpoint matter. This is a prose adaptation.", "beats": [{"title": "Start a short cycle", "example": "A sentry turns every minute."}, {"title": "Add a longer cycle", "example": "A ferry passes every five minutes."}, {"title": "Establish the meeting point", "example": "A bell marks their tenth repetition."}, {"title": "Converge", "example": "The intruder moves when every observer faces away."}], "description": "stratified loops of different lengths all coinciding on a recurring \"gong.\"", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 16, "name": "Sestina", "text": "[Occitan troubadour] — six motifs recur every scene in a fixed rotating permutation.", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "For a strict six-section exercise use orders 123456, 615243, 364125, 532614, 451362, 246531; add a brief closing passage.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose six motifs", "example": "Key, rain, lamp, bread, shoe, name."}, {"title": "Rotate their order", "example": "Each section ends its six small beats with those motifs in a new sequence."}, {"title": "Change their meanings", "example": "The key becomes evidence; the bread becomes an offering."}, {"title": "Gather the motifs", "example": "A short closing passage brings all six together."}], "description": "six motifs recur every scene in a fixed rotating permutation.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 17, "name": "Incremental Repetition", "text": "[folk ballad] — near-identical beats, one variable advanced toward doom.", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "Preserve most of the repeated beat. Change one detail until repetition becomes discovery.", "beats": [{"title": "Repeat an action", "example": "A child knocks and asks to be let in."}, {"title": "Advance one variable", "example": "Each knock comes from a higher floor."}, {"title": "Repeat with consequence", "example": "The household retreats upward."}, {"title": "Break the pattern", "example": "The last knock comes from inside the room."}], "description": "near-identical beats, one variable advanced toward doom.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 18, "name": "The Snowball Circle", "text": "[Inuit-attributed — verify provenance] — circular accretion; ends where it began, fundamentally altered.", "category": "Loops, Refrain & Recurrence", "rule": "Use circular accumulation as an invented exercise. The source's cultural attribution is unverified.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin with one object", "example": "A traveler carries a borrowed cup."}, {"title": "Accumulate encounters", "example": "Each stop adds something to it."}, {"title": "Return to the beginning", "example": "She reaches the lender's house."}, {"title": "Reveal the change", "example": "The cup now carries gifts from the whole village."}], "description": "Accumulate encounters along a route that ends where it began, with changed meaning.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 19, "name": "Ring Composition / Chiasmus", "text": "[ancient, near-universal] — ABCBA fold; the center carries the meaning, the second half inverts the first.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "Echo earlier elements in reverse order; give each return a changed meaning.", "beats": [{"title": "A, establish an image", "example": "A daughter finds the shop bell missing."}, {"title": "B, add a second element", "example": "Her father's tools are wrapped."}, {"title": "C, change the interpretation", "example": "He's adapting the shop for her hearing loss."}, {"title": "B′, revisit", "example": "A tool's grip fits her hand."}, {"title": "A′, return", "example": "The doorway now triggers a light."}], "description": "ABCBA fold; the center carries the meaning, the second half inverts the first.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 20, "name": "Crab Canon / Retrograde", "text": "[Bach] — the second half is the first half exactly reversed.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "Reverse the order of corresponding units exactly. Reversing meaning as well is an optional extra constraint.", "beats": [{"title": "Lay out three actions", "example": "A letter arrives, a lamp goes out, a door closes."}, {"title": "Reach the hinge", "example": "The narrator recognizes the trap."}, {"title": "Reverse the sequence", "example": "The door opens, the lamp lights, the letter leaves."}], "description": "the second half is the first half exactly reversed.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 21, "name": "Pantoum", "text": "[Malay] — each scene reuses the prior's back half; closes on its own opening line. Kin: 19, 20.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "For this prose pantoum, each section's second and fourth beats become the next section's first and third.", "beats": [{"title": "Start with four beats", "example": "Arrival, warning, discovery, refusal."}, {"title": "Carry two forward", "example": "Warning and refusal recur as beats one and three of the next section."}, {"title": "Add new material", "example": "The repeated beats acquire new neighbors."}, {"title": "Close the loop", "example": "The last section returns to opening material."}], "description": "each scene reuses the prior's back half; closes on its own opening line.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 22, "name": "Mugen Noh", "text": "[Japanese dream-theatre] — a stranger revealed as a revenant; part two re-enacts the buried trauma for release.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "Use the encounter and re-enactment as two distinct movements. This is a fiction exercise inspired by dream-theatre.", "beats": [{"title": "Meet a stranger", "example": "A traveler shares shelter with an old ferryman."}, {"title": "Hear the local story", "example": "The ferryman describes a drowned child."}, {"title": "Reveal the identity", "example": "His name appears on the flood memorial."}, {"title": "Re-enact the past", "example": "His account becomes the night of the flood."}, {"title": "Release or leave unresolved", "example": "The traveler speaks the child's name at dawn."}], "description": "a stranger revealed as a revenant; part two re-enacts the buried trauma for release.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 23, "name": "Rashomon Convergence", "text": "[Akutagawa/Kurosawa] — one event restaged 3–4× from conflicting POVs; truth in the negative space.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "Repeat the same event with materially different interpretations. A final authoritative solution is optional.", "beats": [{"title": "Tell the first account", "example": "The guard says the prisoner ran."}, {"title": "Restage the event", "example": "The prisoner says the guard opened the gate."}, {"title": "Add a third account", "example": "A child remembers a shared signal."}, {"title": "Preserve the contradiction", "example": "All agree on the dropped glove, yet disagree about its owner."}], "description": "one event restaged 3–4× from conflicting POVs; truth in the negative space.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 24, "name": "Whodunnit Reverse-Engineer", "text": "— open on the solution; dissect backward through the logic that produced it.", "category": "Mirror, Retrograde & Re-enactment", "rule": "Work backward through reasons, making each earlier clue explain the next inference.", "beats": [{"title": "Show the solution", "example": "The investigator identifies the baker."}, {"title": "Reconstruct the decisive inference", "example": "Only the baker knew the oven was cold."}, {"title": "Trace its evidence", "example": "A receipt recorded the fuel delivery."}, {"title": "Reveal the earlier assumption", "example": "Everyone had mistaken soot for fresh ash."}], "description": "open on the solution; dissect backward through the logic that produced it.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 25, "name": "Fugue State / Dream Logic", "text": "— transitions by sensory association, not chronology or geography.", "category": "Fractured & Drifting Time", "rule": "Every transition needs a sensory or emotional bridge, even when time and place change abruptly.", "beats": [{"title": "Anchor a sensation", "example": "A spoon rings against a cup."}, {"title": "Follow association", "example": "Its sound becomes a school bell."}, {"title": "Shift again", "example": "Chalk dust becomes snow on a hospital sill."}, {"title": "Land on an emotional connection", "example": "Both memories concern someone who never arrived."}], "description": "transitions by sensory association, not chronology or geography.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 26, "name": "Russian Matryoshka", "text": "— story-within-story-within-memory, then pulled all the way back out.", "category": "Fractured & Drifting Time", "rule": "Mark each entrance and exit clearly. Return through the frames in reverse order.", "beats": [{"title": "Open the outer story", "example": "A teacher reads a diary."}, {"title": "Enter its story", "example": "The diarist remembers a sailor."}, {"title": "Enter the innermost account", "example": "The sailor describes a lost child."}, {"title": "Unwind the frames", "example": "The child's name changes the diary, then the teacher's lesson."}], "description": "story-within-story-within-memory, then pulled all the way back out.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 27, "name": "Renga Link-and-Shift", "text": "[Japanese linked verse] — each scene links only to its neighbor and is forbidden to look back.", "category": "Fractured & Drifting Time", "rule": "Each new section links to its immediate predecessor, then moves away. For this exercise, avoid returning to the first image.", "beats": [{"title": "Start one image", "example": "A broken bowl lies in sunlight."}, {"title": "Link through a detail", "example": "Its curve becomes a harbor in the next scene."}, {"title": "Shift again", "example": "A ship's whistle leads to a kitchen kettle."}, {"title": "Continue forward", "example": "The kettle leads to a different household."}], "description": "each scene links only to its neighbor and is forbidden to look back.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 28, "name": "Flâneur Drift", "text": "[French] — a goalless wandering observer accretes atmosphere until a realization lands.", "category": "Fractured & Drifting Time", "rule": "Use attention to organize movement; let the realization grow from what the observer notices.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin wandering", "example": "A woman leaves home without a destination."}, {"title": "Gather observations", "example": "A shuttered cinema reminds her of someone."}, {"title": "Follow curiosity", "example": "A familiar tune takes her down an alley."}, {"title": "Let insight arrive", "example": "She realizes she's retracing her mother's errands."}], "description": "a goalless wandering observer accretes atmosphere until a realization lands.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 29, "name": "Braided POV / Double Helix", "text": "— two threads alternate in bursts that shorten until they violently intersect.", "category": "Braided & Polyphonic", "rule": "Maintain distinct threads and shorten sections as their consequences converge.", "beats": [{"title": "Open thread A", "example": "A courier approaches a checkpoint."}, {"title": "Open thread B", "example": "An analyst deletes a travel record."}, {"title": "Alternate faster", "example": "Their decisions narrow toward the same minute."}, {"title": "Intersect", "example": "The courier's pass vanishes at the scanner."}], "description": "two threads alternate in bursts that shorten until they violently intersect.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 30, "name": "The True Fugue", "text": "[Baroque counterpoint] — a subject imitated by successive voices, overlapping to a stretto collision. Distinct from 25.", "category": "Braided & Polyphonic", "rule": "Repeat an identifiable phrase or action across voices. Vary its meaning before bringing the voices together.", "beats": [{"title": "Introduce a subject", "example": "One worker refuses to sign."}, {"title": "Echo it in another voice", "example": "A supervisor repeats the phrase as an accusation."}, {"title": "Add a third variation", "example": "A child writes it on a wall."}, {"title": "Overlap the voices", "example": "All three versions enter the hearing at once."}], "description": "a subject imitated by successive voices, overlapping to a stretto collision.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 31, "name": "Bunraku Channel-Split", "text": "[Japanese puppet theatre] — description, all dialogue, and emotional \"scoring\" run as three parallel channels.", "category": "Braided & Polyphonic", "rule": "Use clearly labeled or consistently formatted channels. This is an invented prose adaptation, not a description of theatre rules.", "beats": [{"title": "Set the physical channel", "example": "A woman folds a uniform."}, {"title": "Add the speech channel", "example": "She tells her son the trip will be brief."}, {"title": "Add an emotional channel", "example": "A separate refrain counts everything she leaves behind."}, {"title": "Bring channels into friction", "example": "Her cheerful words accompany an empty drawer."}], "description": "Tell one event through separate channels for physical action, speech, and emotional commentary.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 32, "name": "Greek Chorus", "text": "— main action periodically interrupted by a collective commenting voice.", "category": "Braided & Polyphonic", "rule": "Give the collective a consistent voice and let its commentary evolve with events.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin individual action", "example": "A mayor orders a bridge closed."}, {"title": "Let the group respond", "example": "Townspeople recall who built it."}, {"title": "Advance the action", "example": "A family tries to cross."}, {"title": "Return to the collective", "example": "The town's refrain changes as the gate opens."}], "description": "main action periodically interrupted by a collective commenting voice.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 33, "name": "Exquisite Corpse", "text": "[Surrealist] — sections composed blind to each other; the surreal joins are the point.", "category": "Braided & Polyphonic", "rule": "This exercise needs separate contributors or hidden prompts. Solo variant: write disconnected fragments first, then arrange them.", "beats": [{"title": "Write a fragment", "example": "Describe a traveler finding a package."}, {"title": "Hide most of it", "example": "Give a collaborator only the final sentence."}, {"title": "Continue blind", "example": "They write the next fragment from that sentence."}, {"title": "Join and interpret", "example": "Read the combined scene and identify its accidental connection."}], "description": "sections composed blind to each other; the surreal joins are the point.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 34, "name": "French Dialectic", "text": "[Plan Dialectique] — thesis / antithesis / synthesis.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Make both positions plausible. The final position should respond to evidence from both.", "beats": [{"title": "Make a claim", "example": "A council argues the wall keeps the town safe."}, {"title": "Build the counterclaim", "example": "A medic shows what the wall keeps out."}, {"title": "Find a changed position", "example": "They replace the permanent closure with a watched crossing."}], "description": "thesis / antithesis / synthesis.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 35, "name": "Scholastic Quaestio", "text": "[medieval — Aquinas] — objections stacked / sed contra / I answer / point-by-point replies. Sharper kin of 34.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Keep each objection distinct and return to every one after the central response.", "beats": [{"title": "Stack objections", "example": "Three witnesses oppose opening the archive."}, {"title": "Introduce a contrary authority", "example": "A charter guarantees access."}, {"title": "Give the central answer", "example": "The archivist explains why the guarantee applies."}, {"title": "Answer objections individually", "example": "Each concern changes one safeguard."}], "description": "objections stacked / sed contra / I answer / point-by-point replies. Sharper kin of 34.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 36, "name": "The Sugya", "text": "[Talmudic] — question, objection, resolution; the rejected minority opinion is preserved on the page.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "For this adapted exercise, preserve the rejected argument so the debate remains visible.", "beats": [{"title": "Pose a question", "example": "Who owns a recovered letter?"}, {"title": "Test an answer", "example": "The finder claims it."}, {"title": "Challenge it", "example": "The recipient's heir objects."}, {"title": "Develop a resolution", "example": "The court separates custody from ownership."}, {"title": "Retain dissent", "example": "A clerk records the losing interpretation."}], "description": "question, objection, resolution; the rejected minority opinion is preserved on the page.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 37, "name": "Classical Oration", "text": "[Cicero/Quintilian] — exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, refutatio, peroratio.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Give each rhetorical movement a different job. Let the requested action follow from the proof.", "beats": [{"title": "Win attention", "example": "A speaker names the flood's dead."}, {"title": "State the facts", "example": "She recounts the warning failures."}, {"title": "Set out the issues", "example": "Who knew, and who could act?"}, {"title": "Present proof", "example": "She reads the logs."}, {"title": "Refute objections", "example": "She answers the defense."}, {"title": "Close with a request", "example": "She asks for a public inquiry."}], "description": "exordium, narratio, partitio, confirmatio, refutatio, peroratio.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 38, "name": "Eight-Legged Essay / Bagu", "text": "[Chinese] — perfectly symmetrical balanced-pair argument.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Use eight short sections with deliberate paired balance. This is a loose fiction adaptation of a more specific essay tradition.", "beats": [{"title": "Introduce the proposition", "example": "A candidate argues for reopening a school."}, {"title": "Elaborate in paired movements", "example": "Cost meets benefit; precedent meets exception."}, {"title": "Intensify the balanced pairs", "example": "Public duty meets private sacrifice."}, {"title": "Close the argument", "example": "The candidate commits their own home."}], "description": "perfectly symmetrical balanced-pair argument.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 39, "name": "Chreia Elaboration", "text": "[ancient rhetoric] — one maxim expanded through eight fixed moves.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Use eight moves: praise, paraphrase, cause, contrary, analogy, example, testimony, conclusion.", "beats": [{"title": "Name a maxim", "example": "A captain says a debt remembers."}, {"title": "Praise and paraphrase it", "example": "The crew interprets her words."}, {"title": "Give its reason", "example": "A past favor saved her."}, {"title": "Test its opposite", "example": "An abandoned debtor refused aid."}, {"title": "Add analogy and example", "example": "A repaired net and a rescued sailor echo the claim."}, {"title": "Cite testimony and close", "example": "A survivor confirms the cost."}], "description": "one maxim expanded through eight fixed moves.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 40, "name": "Cross-Examination Spiral", "text": "— broad questions circling inward, accelerating, to a fatal contradiction.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Each question should remove an escape route. Establish the decisive fact before using it.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin broadly", "example": "Ask a witness about the workday."}, {"title": "Restrict the window", "example": "Ask who remained after closing."}, {"title": "Pin down one claim", "example": "Ask which door they used."}, {"title": "Expose contradiction", "example": "That door was sealed before they arrived."}], "description": "broad questions circling inward, accelerating, to a fatal contradiction.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 41, "name": "Burden of Proof", "text": "— begin from assumed guilt; systematically dismantle it.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Give each beat a specific claim to test. End with a changed assessment, not just generalized doubt.", "beats": [{"title": "State the accusation", "example": "A nurse is blamed for missing medicine."}, {"title": "Challenge one assumption", "example": "The count was taken before delivery."}, {"title": "Remove another support", "example": "The key was shared."}, {"title": "Reframe the evidence", "example": "A transfer log accounts for the stock."}], "description": "begin from assumed guilt; systematically dismantle it.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 42, "name": "Objection Volley", "text": "— staccato: every rhythm violently interrupted by a counter.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Each interruption must alter the available next move. Let the rhythm reflect the struggle for control.", "beats": [{"title": "Make a move", "example": "A negotiator offers a crossing."}, {"title": "Interrupt it", "example": "The rival demands a hostage."}, {"title": "Counter immediately", "example": "The negotiator names the rival's missing brother."}, {"title": "Force a new position", "example": "Both stop bargaining to compare evidence."}], "description": "staccato: every rhythm violently interrupted by a counter.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 43, "name": "Deposition Breakdown", "text": "— clinical Q&A transcript alternating with the messy remembered reality.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Keep transcript and memory distinguishable. Make their differences reveal something consequential.", "beats": [{"title": "Ask a clinical question", "example": "What time did you arrive?"}, {"title": "Enter remembered experience", "example": "The witness recalls searching for a shoe in the dark."}, {"title": "Return to the transcript", "example": "They give a precise, misleading time."}, {"title": "Let the mismatch widen", "example": "A simple question brings back what the record excludes."}], "description": "clinical Q&A transcript alternating with the messy remembered reality.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 44, "name": "Evidence Chain", "text": "— POV follows one artifact hand to hand; stakes ride on who holds it.", "category": "Argument, Law & Dialectic", "rule": "Every handoff must change risk, knowledge, or value. Keep the artifact traceable.", "beats": [{"title": "Place the object", "example": "A child finds a medal."}, {"title": "Transfer possession", "example": "A dealer buys it."}, {"title": "Change its significance", "example": "A veteran recognizes its number."}, {"title": "Deliver its consequence", "example": "A court receives the medal as evidence."}], "description": "POV follows one artifact hand to hand; stakes ride on who holds it.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 45, "name": "Recursive Loop / Iteration", "text": "[computational] — repeat the scenario, accumulate knowledge, until the loop breaks.", "category": "Code & Systems Logic", "rule": "Keep the obstacle recognizable while knowledge accumulates. The final attempt uses what earlier failures taught.", "beats": [{"title": "Set the failed attempt", "example": "A traveler misses the last ferry."}, {"title": "Repeat with knowledge", "example": "She arrives earlier but lacks a ticket."}, {"title": "Retain another lesson", "example": "She buys one in advance."}, {"title": "Break the loop", "example": "She realizes the ferryman needs help launching."}], "description": "repeat the scenario, accumulate knowledge, until the loop breaks.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 46, "name": "Version Control / Git Pacing", "text": "— the text visibly overwrites itself; drafts replace drafts in real time.", "category": "Code & Systems Logic", "rule": "Make revisions visible and meaningful; use clear version labels so readers can follow what changed.", "beats": [{"title": "Present a first account", "example": "A report says the gate stayed shut."}, {"title": "Show a revision", "example": "The report changes shut to secured."}, {"title": "Expose the edit", "example": "A witness annotates the erased line."}, {"title": "Leave a consequential version", "example": "The final text admits someone was inside."}], "description": "the text visibly overwrites itself; drafts replace drafts in real time.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 47, "name": "Boolean Trap (If/Then)", "text": "— progress only when binary conditions are met; failure is a dead end.", "category": "Code & Systems Logic", "rule": "Declare understandable conditions. Avoid inventing a new requirement after the character meets the old one.", "beats": [{"title": "State a condition", "example": "The door opens only if both keys turn."}, {"title": "Meet half the condition", "example": "One traveler finds a key."}, {"title": "Expose the second requirement", "example": "Its owner refuses to help."}, {"title": "Satisfy or fail the rule", "example": "Cooperation opens the door at a personal cost."}], "description": "progress only when binary conditions are met; failure is a dead end.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 48, "name": "Stack Trace", "text": "— begin at the crash; retrace the exact cascade back to the origin fault.", "category": "Code & Systems Logic", "rule": "Each backward step must explain the failure immediately before it. Finish at a cause that changes judgment.", "beats": [{"title": "Show the failure", "example": "The city's lights die."}, {"title": "Trace the immediate cause", "example": "A relay overheated."}, {"title": "Find the earlier cause", "example": "Its cooling pump had stopped."}, {"title": "Reach the origin", "example": "A routine repair diverted its supply."}], "description": "begin at the crash; retrace the exact cascade back to the origin fault.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 49, "name": "Memory Leak", "text": "— the narration degrades, fragmenting until only raw emotion remains.", "category": "Code & Systems Logic", "rule": "Design what deteriorates and what remains legible. Keep enough pattern for readers to feel the loss.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin with control", "example": "A narrator records names and times precisely."}, {"title": "Lose a small capacity", "example": "A name becomes a description."}, {"title": "Lose connective tissue", "example": "Time markers and explanations fall away."}, {"title": "Preserve one emotional remnant", "example": "A repeated plea survives the broken account."}], "description": "the narration degrades, fragmenting until only raw emotion remains.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 50, "name": "Epistolary Mosaic", "text": "— in-world documents arranged so the reader connects the dots. Kin: 51.", "category": "Mosaic, Collage & Montage", "rule": "Give documents different sources and purposes. Their relationship should create the discovery.", "beats": [{"title": "Introduce one document", "example": "A visitor log records an arrival."}, {"title": "Add a different source", "example": "A letter denies the meeting."}, {"title": "Expose a gap", "example": "A photograph shows an unlisted guest."}, {"title": "Let the reader connect", "example": "A receipt places that guest at the exit."}], "description": "in-world documents arranged so the reader connects the dots.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 51, "name": "The Cento", "text": "[late-antique] — built entirely from borrowed/quoted fragments repurposed into new meaning. Kin: 50.", "category": "Mosaic, Collage & Montage", "rule": "Use your own writing, public-domain material, or text you have permission to reuse; build the exercise entirely from those fragments.", "beats": [{"title": "Gather permitted fragments", "example": "Collect lines from your own notes."}, {"title": "Choose a new situation", "example": "Arrange them as a farewell."}, {"title": "Make joins meaningful", "example": "A joke becomes an accusation beside another line."}, {"title": "Close with a transformed fragment", "example": "The opening phrase now sounds like forgiveness."}], "description": "built entirely from borrowed/quoted fragments repurposed into new meaning.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 52, "name": "The Cut-Up", "text": "[Dada → Burroughs] — two source texts sliced and interleaved for forced collision. Kin: 53.", "category": "Mosaic, Collage & Montage", "rule": "Let juxtaposition produce meaning. Editing for readability is allowed in this adapted exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Create two source passages", "example": "Write a weather report and a breakup letter."}, {"title": "Cut them into short units", "example": "Keep enough syntax to remain readable."}, {"title": "Interleave", "example": "Place a forecast between two personal admissions."}, {"title": "Select the productive collisions", "example": "Retain joins that suggest a shared emotional weather."}], "description": "two source texts sliced and interleaved for forced collision.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 53, "name": "Kuleshov Effect", "text": "[Soviet montage] — unrelated scenes juxtaposed without transition; the reader infers the link. Kin: 52.", "category": "Mosaic, Collage & Montage", "rule": "Avoid explaining the connection. Let the order of images guide the reader's interpretation.", "beats": [{"title": "Show an ambiguous image", "example": "A woman watches a doorway."}, {"title": "Cut to another image", "example": "A child's shoes sit beside a bed."}, {"title": "Return to the first image", "example": "Her hand rests on the lock."}, {"title": "Shift the inferred meaning", "example": "A moving van arrives."}], "description": "unrelated scenes juxtaposed without transition; the reader infers the link.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 54, "name": "Kamishibai Tableau", "text": "[Japanese paper theatre] — freeze into discrete, intensely detailed snapshots. Inverse of 72.", "category": "Mosaic, Collage & Montage", "rule": "Use discrete still moments; let changes between them imply the missing action.", "beats": [{"title": "Freeze a first tableau", "example": "A family stands at a station."}, {"title": "Advance to a changed image", "example": "One suitcase lies open on the platform."}, {"title": "Advance again", "example": "Only the father remains beneath the clock."}, {"title": "Finish on a decisive image", "example": "A child's ticket rests in his palm."}], "description": "freeze into discrete, intensely detailed snapshots.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 55, "name": "Brechtian Verfremdungseffekt", "text": "[German] — deliberately break immersion with sterile facts to force objectivity.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Use interruption to invite judgment about the situation. Emotional distance is a tool, not a guaranteed reader response.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin an absorbing scene", "example": "A worker begs for one more shift."}, {"title": "Interrupt with a fact", "example": "Insert the factory's profit per hour."}, {"title": "Resume the exchange", "example": "The manager cites necessity."}, {"title": "Expose the arrangement", "example": "Address what makes this choice possible."}], "description": "Interrupt absorption in the scene so readers can examine how its social situation works.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 56, "name": "The Tristram Shandy Engine", "text": "[Sterne] — progress by non-progress; digression metastasizes into the real content.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Each detour should enrich the voice or central concern. Keep the promised event visible enough to orient readers.", "beats": [{"title": "Promise an event", "example": "The narrator will explain the duel."}, {"title": "Digress for a reason", "example": "The sword requires its own history."}, {"title": "Let digression branch", "example": "Its maker leads to a family feud."}, {"title": "Reveal the real subject", "example": "The delay exposes why the narrator fears the duel."}], "description": "progress by non-progress; digression metastasizes into the real content.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 57, "name": "The Anatomy", "text": "[Menippean — Burton] — stop narrating to dissect one phenomenon exhaustively.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Organize by categories rather than chronology. Make the analysis change our view of the narrator.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose a phenomenon", "example": "A narrator investigates the town's habit of silence."}, {"title": "Classify its forms", "example": "Polite, fearful, exhausted, complicit."}, {"title": "Examine cases", "example": "Each kind appears in a brief incident."}, {"title": "Return to the story", "example": "The narrator's own silence becomes the final case."}], "description": "stop narrating to dissect one phenomenon exhaustively.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 58, "name": "The Lipogram / Oulipo Constraint", "text": "[French] — a hidden rule warps every sentence; the enforced absence mirrors a thematic one.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Choose a precise lexical constraint and obey it. A strict lipogram omits a chosen letter throughout.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose an absence", "example": "Write a reunion without using personal names."}, {"title": "Establish a workaround", "example": "Identify people through gestures and relationships."}, {"title": "Let the rule shape feeling", "example": "Avoided names expose old distance."}, {"title": "Reach a charged ending", "example": "A character almost speaks one."}], "description": "a hidden rule warps every sentence; the enforced absence mirrors a thematic one.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 59, "name": "The Rhopalic Snowball", "text": "[Oulipo/ancient] — each unit one element longer; visible swelling toward rupture.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "For this exercise count words per paragraph: 1, 2, 3, and onward. Other rhopalic constraints can use different units.", "beats": [{"title": "Set a small unit", "example": "Start with a one-word paragraph."}, {"title": "Grow deliberately", "example": "Add one word to each following paragraph."}, {"title": "Accumulate consequences", "example": "Each expansion admits another person or fact."}, {"title": "End at the planned size", "example": "The longest paragraph contains the revelation."}], "description": "each unit one element longer; visible swelling toward rupture.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 60, "name": "Abecedarian / Acrostic", "text": "[Hebrew scripture] — an external fixed sequence (the alphabet) governs the beats.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Pick the sequence before drafting. The ordering constraint should shape actual content.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose a fixed sequence", "example": "Use letters A through F as section initials."}, {"title": "Assign each a story beat", "example": "Arrival, bargain, concealment, discovery, escape, farewell."}, {"title": "Write within the order", "example": "Make each section move the scene."}, {"title": "End on the final assigned letter", "example": "Complete the local change."}], "description": "an external fixed sequence (the alphabet) governs the beats.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 61, "name": "Champu", "text": "[Sanskrit/Kannada] — scheduled alternation of flat prose and heightened verse at the peaks.", "category": "Constraint & Frame-Breaking", "rule": "Make the prose/verse alternation legible and purposeful. This is an adapted mixed-form exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin in prose", "example": "A traveler describes packing."}, {"title": "Shift to verse at intensity", "example": "A short stanza addresses the absent companion."}, {"title": "Return to prose", "example": "The ferry boarding proceeds."}, {"title": "End with another verse peak", "example": "The coastline disappears."}], "description": "scheduled alternation of flat prose and heightened verse at the peaks.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 62, "name": "The Ghazal", "text": "[Arabic/Persian/Urdu] — self-contained scenes unified by a refrain-image and one mood, never by plot.", "category": "Non-Causal & Mood-Unified", "rule": "Use self-contained miniature scenes linked by a refrain and mood. This is a prose adaptation, not a strict ghazal.", "beats": [{"title": "Write an independent scene", "example": "A mother sets two cups on a table."}, {"title": "Write another", "example": "A sailor watches an empty berth."}, {"title": "Add a third", "example": "A gardener leaves a gate open."}, {"title": "Return a refrain", "example": "Each ends with the same phrase about waiting."}], "description": "Arrange self-contained miniature scenes around a recurring phrase and emotional atmosphere.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 63, "name": "The Qasīda", "text": "[pre-Islamic Arabic] — ruined-campsite lament → brutal journey → arrival at the petition/boast.", "category": "Non-Causal & Mood-Unified", "rule": "Let remembered loss motivate the journey and final address. This is an adapted three-movement exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin with the abandoned place", "example": "A traveler finds the family's campsite deserted."}, {"title": "Cross the intervening world", "example": "The journey tests what they remember."}, {"title": "Arrive with a purpose", "example": "They ask a ruler for shelter for the missing family."}], "description": "ruined-campsite lament → brutal journey → arrival at the petition/boast.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 64, "name": "Magic Realism Anchor", "text": "[Latin American] — the impossible stated in the register of the mundane, never explained.", "category": "Non-Causal & Mood-Unified", "rule": "Keep the narrative register matter-of-fact. This is a tonal exercise; you still need a sequence of meaningful events.", "beats": [{"title": "State the impossible plainly", "example": "Every Tuesday the dead take the bus."}, {"title": "Show ordinary logistics", "example": "A driver checks their passes."}, {"title": "Develop a human concern", "example": "One passenger can't find her son."}, {"title": "Let consequences stand", "example": "The driver changes the route."}], "description": "the impossible stated in the register of the mundane, never explained.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 65, "name": "Liturgy of the Hours", "text": "[monastic] — the eight canonical offices, each its own mood, clock the chapter.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Use eight time anchors for this exercise. Treat this as a secular scheduling adaptation.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose recurring hours", "example": "A caretaker records eight visits to a bedside."}, {"title": "Vary the task at each", "example": "Water, medicine, reading, rest."}, {"title": "Let the intervals carry change", "example": "The patient begins recognizing the voice."}, {"title": "Complete the daily cycle", "example": "The final visit repeats the first with new intimacy."}], "description": "the eight canonical offices, each its own mood, clock the chapter.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 66, "name": "The Seder Order", "text": "[Passover Haggadah] — fixed ritual steps with an embedded \"why is tonight different\" catechism.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Borrow the structural principle of ordered actions plus questions; use your own fictional ritual.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish an invented ritual", "example": "A family prepares an annual meal."}, {"title": "Follow its fixed order", "example": "An empty chair is set before food arrives."}, {"title": "Ask why", "example": "A child questions the missing place setting."}, {"title": "Let the answer change participation", "example": "The family invites a former enemy."}], "description": "fixed ritual steps with an embedded \"why is tonight different\" catechism.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 67, "name": "The Hexagram", "text": "[I Ching] — six broken/unbroken beats read as one situation; a \"changing line\" mutates it into the sequel.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Create your own binary scene code. This exercise isn't a divination method or a traditional reading.", "beats": [{"title": "Define six paired states", "example": "Open/closed, near/far, known/unknown and three more."}, {"title": "Build six beats", "example": "Each expresses one chosen state."}, {"title": "Change one line", "example": "A closed gate becomes open."}, {"title": "Reinterpret the whole", "example": "That change turns refuge into exposure."}], "description": "six broken/unbroken beats read as one situation; a \"changing line\" mutates it into the sequel.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 68, "name": "The Calendar Round", "text": "[Mesoamerican] — two clocks of different lengths mesh; catastrophe lands on their alignment.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Track two cycles accurately. This invented scheduling exercise doesn't reproduce a cultural calendar.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish cycle A", "example": "An inspection happens every three days."}, {"title": "Establish cycle B", "example": "A supply boat arrives every five."}, {"title": "Show both progressing", "example": "Small problems accumulate between visits."}, {"title": "Converge", "example": "On day fifteen the inspector meets the smuggled passenger."}], "description": "two clocks of different lengths mesh; catastrophe lands on their alignment.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 69, "name": "The Ta'ziyeh", "text": "[Persian passion play] — a communal processional march toward a liturgically foreknown death. Kin: 87.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Use foreknown tragedy and communal witnessing as dramatic principles; don't imitate sacred performance details.", "beats": [{"title": "Announce the foreknown end", "example": "A town gathers for a condemned captain's final walk."}, {"title": "Move through public stations", "example": "Each encounter reveals a bond."}, {"title": "Allow choices within the ending", "example": "The captain decides whom to forgive."}, {"title": "Arrive", "example": "The crowd carries the final testimony forward."}], "description": "a communal processional march toward a liturgically foreknown death.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 70, "name": "Restoration Arc / Hózhǫ́", "text": "[Diné — sacred; adapt the logic, not the rite] — disorder → fourfold movement → restored balance, sealed by a repeated closing cadence.", "category": "Ritual, Liturgy & Calendar", "rule": "Use an invented repair arc. Avoid presenting these invented steps as a Diné ceremony.", "beats": [{"title": "Show damaged balance", "example": "Neighbors no longer share a well."}, {"title": "Attempt repair through four movements", "example": "Listen, acknowledge, repay, rebuild."}, {"title": "Demonstrate a restored relation", "example": "Both families draw water."}, {"title": "Repeat a closing phrase", "example": "The same words now sound earned."}], "description": "An invented repair arc that moves from damaged relationships toward restored balance.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 71, "name": "Songlines", "text": "[Aboriginal Australian] — sequence dictated by a geographic route; to tell the story is to walk the land.", "category": "Spatial & Cartographic", "rule": "Use a fictional geographic memory-route; don't claim it reproduces Aboriginal songlines or cultural knowledge.", "beats": [{"title": "Set a route", "example": "A courier follows five landmarks."}, {"title": "Tie each place to a memory", "example": "A bend recalls a promise."}, {"title": "Let geography determine order", "example": "The only safe crossing forces a difficult stop."}, {"title": "Arrive carrying the route's meaning", "example": "The destination changes with what was remembered."}], "description": "Let a fictional route organize the sequence of events and memories.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 72, "name": "The Emaki Handscroll", "text": "[Japanese, iji-dōzu] — one continuous lateral pan, no scene breaks, the figure recurring across moments. Inverse of 54.", "category": "Spatial & Cartographic", "rule": "Maintain a continuous descriptive movement. Signal elapsed time without conventional scene breaks.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin with a moving view", "example": "Follow a festival street from the river."}, {"title": "Let action enter the frame", "example": "A child runs ahead of the procession."}, {"title": "Advance continuously", "example": "Shops close as the procession passes."}, {"title": "End at the destination", "example": "The child reappears at the gate."}], "description": "one continuous lateral pan, no scene breaks, the figure recurring across moments.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 73, "name": "The Mandala", "text": "[Hindu/Buddhist] — penetrate nested concentric courts past cardinal guardians toward a central revelation. Inverse of 76.", "category": "Spatial & Cartographic", "rule": "Use invented concentric spaces and escalating access conditions; this is a spatial fiction exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Enter the outer ring", "example": "A visitor reaches a guarded garden."}, {"title": "Pass inward", "example": "Each threshold asks for a different concession."}, {"title": "Reach the center", "example": "The final room contains their own lost work."}, {"title": "Interpret the journey", "example": "What they surrendered changes its value."}], "description": "penetrate nested concentric courts past cardinal guardians toward a central revelation.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 74, "name": "The Memory Palace", "text": "[classical loci] — walk a remembered architecture; each room stores one beat.", "category": "Spatial & Cartographic", "rule": "Keep the architecture stable so spatial movement organizes the memories.", "beats": [{"title": "Enter a remembered building", "example": "A woman recalls her childhood kitchen."}, {"title": "Move room by room", "example": "Each place releases one relevant memory."}, {"title": "Reach a locked space", "example": "The final room holds an avoided event."}, {"title": "Leave changed", "example": "She redraws the house with its missing door."}], "description": "walk a remembered architecture; each room stores one beat.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 75, "name": "The Unicursal Labyrinth", "text": "[Chartres] — a single inevitable switchbacking path to the center and back; no wrong turns.", "category": "Spatial & Cartographic", "rule": "Use one continuous route with no branching choices. Change interpretation during the return.", "beats": [{"title": "Enter one winding path", "example": "A pilgrim follows a marked corridor."}, {"title": "Approach and recede", "example": "The center appears near, then distant."}, {"title": "Reach the center", "example": "A simple message awaits."}, {"title": "Retrace the route", "example": "Familiar turns feel different after reading it."}], "description": "a single inevitable switchbacking path to the center and back; no wrong turns.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 76, "name": "Perimeter Breach", "text": "— concentric defenses failing one by one from the outside in. Inverse of 73.", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "Each failure should force a changed position or decision. Make the geography easy to follow.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish the defenses", "example": "A shelter has a fence, doors, and inner vault."}, {"title": "Lose the outer layer", "example": "The fence falls."}, {"title": "Lose the next", "example": "A damaged door forces retreat."}, {"title": "Test the last defense", "example": "The vault opens only for someone outside."}], "description": "concentric defenses failing one by one from the outside in.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 77, "name": "Quarantine Lockdown / Shrinking Box", "text": "— the navigable space contracts scene by scene.", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "At each beat remove a space and show what option disappears with it.", "beats": [{"title": "Start with usable space", "example": "Survivors occupy an entire ship."}, {"title": "Close an area", "example": "Water claims the lower deck."}, {"title": "Contract again", "example": "Smoke blocks the stairs."}, {"title": "Force a final choice", "example": "Two people reach a hatch only one can hold open."}], "description": "the navigable space contracts scene by scene.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 78, "name": "Fog of War", "text": "— information starved to terrifying flashes; jagged, confused pacing. Kin: 79.", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "Limit information through a consistent viewpoint. Keep the character's immediate action comprehensible.", "beats": [{"title": "Give partial information", "example": "A soldier hears an unfamiliar engine."}, {"title": "Make a decision from it", "example": "She redirects the patrol."}, {"title": "Reveal conflicting evidence", "example": "The map marks an empty road."}, {"title": "Clarify one local fact", "example": "The engine belongs to a rescue vehicle."}], "description": "information starved to terrifying flashes; jagged, confused pacing.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 79, "name": "Sensory Deprivation Box", "text": "— total isolation; structured purely by memory, hallucination, dread. Kin: 78.", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "Choose which senses remain available. Let each interpretation lead to a concrete test or choice.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish isolation", "example": "A person wakes in a lightless room."}, {"title": "Build a sensory measure", "example": "Drips become a makeshift clock."}, {"title": "Let memory intrude", "example": "A familiar rhythm suggests home."}, {"title": "Test perception", "example": "The rhythm answers a deliberate tap."}], "description": "total isolation; structured purely by memory, hallucination, dread.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 80, "name": "Attrition Cascade", "text": "— tension mapped to a dwindling counter (ammo, oxygen, daylight, sanity).", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "Track the resource consistently and attach a changing consequence to each loss.", "beats": [{"title": "Set the resource count", "example": "A climber has four working batteries."}, {"title": "Spend one to act", "example": "A radio call uses the first."}, {"title": "Make the cost harder", "example": "A searchlight consumes another."}, {"title": "Face the final allocation", "example": "The last battery can signal or warm a companion."}], "description": "tension mapped to a dwindling counter (ammo, oxygen, daylight, sanity).", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 81, "name": "Chain of Command", "text": "— an order or piece of intel cascades up/down a hierarchy, distorting as it passes.", "category": "Military, Survival & Tension", "rule": "Give each distortion a believable cause, such as fear, haste, or self-interest.", "beats": [{"title": "Issue an order", "example": "A captain says delay the crossing until dawn."}, {"title": "Pass it onward", "example": "A lieutenant abbreviates it to hold the crossing."}, {"title": "Distort through incentives", "example": "A sentry interprets hold as arrest travelers."}, {"title": "Reveal the consequence", "example": "A medical convoy is detained."}], "description": "an order or piece of intel cascades up/down a hierarchy, distorting as it passes.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 82, "name": "Rasa Engineering", "text": "[Indian aesthetics] — the chapter built as a delivery machine for one dominant emotion.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Build a scene around one dominant emotional experience while allowing contrast. This is a simplified exercise inspired by rasa.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose a dominant feeling", "example": "Wonder."}, {"title": "Prepare its conditions", "example": "A child enters an unfamiliar observatory."}, {"title": "Vary its expression", "example": "Scale, sound, and human kindness deepen the feeling."}, {"title": "End with a resonant action", "example": "She leaves a question for the next visitor."}], "description": "the chapter built as a delivery machine for one dominant emotion.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 83, "name": "Stages of Grief", "text": "— denial/anger/bargaining/depression/acceptance as the literal pacing beats.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Use these as optional fictional beats; real grief doesn't follow a fixed universal sequence.", "beats": [{"title": "Deny a loss", "example": "A character still sets a second place."}, {"title": "Feel anger", "example": "They accuse a friend of clearing the room."}, {"title": "Bargain", "example": "They preserve the last voicemail."}, {"title": "Meet desolation", "example": "The recording ends."}, {"title": "Make room for reality", "example": "They eat with the friend."}], "description": "An optional fictional sequence of contrasting responses to loss; it isn’t a model for how everyone grieves.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 84, "name": "Cognitive Dissonance", "text": "— interior monologue continuously contradicts external action and speech.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Pair each internal claim with an observable contradiction. Let the gap evolve.", "beats": [{"title": "State a private belief", "example": "A host thinks the visitor is harmless."}, {"title": "Contradict it through action", "example": "They hide the knives."}, {"title": "Rationalize the mismatch", "example": "They call it tidying."}, {"title": "Force recognition", "example": "They lock the door and keep the key."}], "description": "interior monologue continuously contradicts external action and speech.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 85, "name": "Paranoia Pendulum", "text": "— rhythmic swings between blinding trust and absolute dread.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Each swing needs new evidence. Avoid suspicion that appears without a trigger.", "beats": [{"title": "Offer reassurance", "example": "A guide returns a lost wallet."}, {"title": "Introduce doubt", "example": "One photograph is missing."}, {"title": "Restore trust", "example": "The guide says it fell out and returns it."}, {"title": "Deepen uncertainty", "example": "The photograph has a new inscription."}], "description": "rhythmic swings between blinding trust and absolute dread.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 86, "name": "Intrusive Thought Override", "text": "— a standard scene repeatedly hijacked by one unignorable thought until it derails.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Repeat one specific mental interruption, changing its force until it alters behavior.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin an ordinary task", "example": "A clerk interviews an applicant."}, {"title": "Intrude with one thought", "example": "The applicant has her brother's hands."}, {"title": "Resume imperfectly", "example": "She asks the wrong question."}, {"title": "Let intrusion redirect action", "example": "She abandons the form to ask about his family."}], "description": "a standard scene repeatedly hijacked by one unignorable thought until it derails.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 87, "name": "Terminal Clarity", "text": "— the calm, observant pacing of someone who already knows they've lost. Kin: 69.", "category": "Emotional & Psychological Engines", "rule": "Shift suspense toward what the character does with remaining agency.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish the known outcome", "example": "A stranded sailor knows rescue won't arrive."}, {"title": "Attend to particulars", "example": "He repairs a child's toy."}, {"title": "Settle a relationship", "example": "He records an apology."}, {"title": "Choose a final act", "example": "He leaves the toy where it can be found."}], "description": "the calm, observant pacing of someone who already knows they've lost.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 88, "name": "Monogatari Dialogue Matrix", "text": "[Japanese] — hyper-fast layered conversation doing the work of a brawl.", "category": "Dialogue & Verbal Combat", "rule": "Make each exchange change knowledge, leverage, or intimacy. This is an invented dialogue exercise.", "beats": [{"title": "Start a verbal contest", "example": "Two friends debate a borrowed coat."}, {"title": "Layer meanings", "example": "Their jokes reveal a hidden debt."}, {"title": "Change the stakes", "example": "One admits why they needed it."}, {"title": "Resolve through speech", "example": "The other offers the coat with a new condition."}], "description": "hyper-fast layered conversation doing the work of a brawl.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 89, "name": "Flyting", "text": "[Norse/Scots] — a ritual escalating insult-duel; combat by speech, in rounds. Kin: 90.", "category": "Dialogue & Verbal Combat", "rule": "Use distinct rounds and a visible audience response; let escalation reveal character.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish the duel", "example": "Two rivals agree to trade insults before a crowd."}, {"title": "Escalate in rounds", "example": "Boasts become personal accusations."}, {"title": "Cross a boundary", "example": "One names a shared loss."}, {"title": "Decide the outcome", "example": "The other wins by refusing the next insult."}], "description": "a ritual escalating insult-duel; combat by speech, in rounds.", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 90, "name": "Katajjaq Duel", "text": "[Inuit throat-song] — two interlocking voices racing until one breaks. Kin: 89.", "category": "Dialogue & Verbal Combat", "rule": "This is an invented verbal pattern inspired by interlocking performance, not a representation of Inuit throat-singing.", "beats": [{"title": "Set two alternating voices", "example": "Two siblings repeat competing versions of a promise."}, {"title": "Interlock the phrases", "example": "Each response begins with the other's final word."}, {"title": "Accelerate the exchange", "example": "Replies shorten."}, {"title": "Break the pattern", "example": "One voice falls silent at the remembered truth."}], "description": "An invented dialogue constraint in which alternating voices interlock and accelerate.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 91, "name": "Commedia Lazzi", "text": "[Italian] — a loose frame punctuated by recurring stock set-piece \"bits.\"", "category": "Dialogue & Verbal Combat", "rule": "Keep the comic action recognizable and vary its stakes. The final recurrence should affect the scene.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish a recurring bit", "example": "A servant keeps replacing a broken chair."}, {"title": "Repeat under new conditions", "example": "The chair interrupts a proposal."}, {"title": "Escalate the routine", "example": "It collapses during an accusation."}, {"title": "Make the bit consequential", "example": "Its hidden compartment spills evidence."}], "description": "a loose frame punctuated by recurring stock set-piece \"bits.\"", "level": "exploratory"}, {"id": 92, "name": "Tarot Spread (Celtic Cross)", "text": "— ten fixed positions, each meaning framing its scene.", "category": "Chance, Game & Grids", "rule": "Use ten assigned positions as an outline grid. The exercise doesn't predict outcomes.", "beats": [{"title": "Map the present situation", "example": "A visitor arrives at a locked house."}, {"title": "Add obstacle and foundation", "example": "A rival holds the key; an old promise explains why."}, {"title": "Contrast past and possible future", "example": "Earlier betrayal meets imagined reconciliation."}, {"title": "Add self, surroundings, hopes, outcome", "example": "Each position reveals a different influence."}], "description": "ten fixed positions, each meaning framing its scene.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 93, "name": "Gyan Chaupar", "text": "[Indian — ancestor of Snakes & Ladders] — moral-board traversal; virtues are sudden ascents, vices sudden plunges.", "category": "Chance, Game & Grids", "rule": "Design explicit advances and setbacks linked to character choices; use your own fictional game board.", "beats": [{"title": "Start on a simple track", "example": "A traveler seeks entry to a city."}, {"title": "Reward a choice", "example": "Helping a stranger opens a shortcut."}, {"title": "Make another choice costly", "example": "A lie sends them back to the checkpoint."}, {"title": "Reach a changed position", "example": "They confess and accept a slower route."}], "description": "moral-board traversal; virtues are sudden ascents, vices sudden plunges.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 94, "name": "Isekai / Liminal Transition", "text": "[anime] — the disorienting rules of crossing from a known reality into an alien state.", "category": "Tonal & Liminal", "rule": "Teach new rules through consequences. Give the character one concrete decision within the unfamiliar world.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish familiar rules", "example": "A commuter boards the usual train."}, {"title": "Cross the threshold", "example": "The next stop has no sky."}, {"title": "Test a new rule", "example": "Speaking a name changes the station."}, {"title": "Choose an adaptation", "example": "They use a forgotten name to find a way forward."}], "description": "the disorienting rules of crossing from a known reality into an alien state.", "level": "grounded"}, {"id": 95, "name": "Perplexity Gradient", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A static exercise adapted from Perplexity Gradient. Linguistic surprise can be authored on paper; this site doesn't measure model probabilities.", "beats": [{"title": "Begin with familiar language", "example": "A narrator gives a calm weather report."}, {"title": "Introduce unusual choices", "example": "Their comparisons grow increasingly strange."}, {"title": "Disrupt the pattern", "example": "Syntax starts reflecting their disorientation."}, {"title": "Preserve an anchor", "example": "One repeated ordinary sentence helps readers follow."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 96, "name": "Collapsing Superposition", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A branching page exercise adapted from Collapsing Superposition. Live generation would need an additional system; this site provides the outline only.", "beats": [{"title": "Write a shared opening", "example": "A traveler reaches two possible homes."}, {"title": "Sketch incompatible branches", "example": "In one she is welcomed; in the other she's unknown."}, {"title": "Select one reading path", "example": "Let a reader choose a question."}, {"title": "Leave a trace of the other path", "example": "An unexplained key survives."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 97, "name": "Stylometric Possession", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A page exercise adapted from Stylometric Possession. Use your own invented voices; this site doesn't ingest or analyze a reader's writing.", "beats": [{"title": "Establish a narrator's voice", "example": "A formal clerk begins a report."}, {"title": "Introduce another voice", "example": "A witness's phrases enter the account."}, {"title": "Increase the borrowing", "example": "Sentence rhythms begin to match the witness."}, {"title": "Reveal the consequence", "example": "The clerk signs with the witness's habitual farewell."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 98, "name": "Forgetting Engine", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A static adaptation of Forgetting Engine. Simulate forgetting by hiding notes; this site doesn't erase drafts or control a model's memory.", "beats": [{"title": "List remembered facts", "example": "A traveler records five details about home."}, {"title": "Remove one anchor", "example": "Write the next passage without access to one detail."}, {"title": "Retain a chosen memory", "example": "One fact survives every revision."}, {"title": "End with a gap", "example": "The missing detail changes a decision."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 99, "name": "Latent Geodesic", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A human-authored adaptation of Latent Geodesic. This site doesn't compute embeddings or claim a unique midpoint between ideas.", "beats": [{"title": "Choose distant concepts", "example": "Begin with grief and end with duty."}, {"title": "Find a bridge", "example": "A repeated household ritual connects them."}, {"title": "Turn bridges into scenes", "example": "Each scene grows from the previous concept."}, {"title": "Arrive with meaning changed", "example": "Duty becomes a way of remembering."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 100, "name": "The Crucible", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A role-based drafting exercise adapted from The Crucible. You can roleplay it with people; this site doesn't run autonomous agents.", "beats": [{"title": "Give characters private goals", "example": "A witness wants safety; a lawyer wants a name."}, {"title": "Limit their knowledge", "example": "Write separate notes for each."}, {"title": "Let choices collide", "example": "Each response follows the speaker's own goal."}, {"title": "Curate the outcome", "example": "Select the exchange that changes the case."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}, {"id": 101, "name": "Redaction Engine", "text": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "category": "Experimental page adaptations", "rule": "A fictional disclosure exercise adapted from Redaction Engine. The site's challenge generator has no live AI or declassification system.", "beats": [{"title": "Define the concealed fact", "example": "A clerk knows who signed the order."}, {"title": "Establish disclosure rules", "example": "They can reveal dates before identities."}, {"title": "Ask successive questions", "example": "Each answer changes the next request."}, {"title": "Reach a consequential boundary", "example": "The final answer exposes a relationship."}], "description": "A page-based exercise adapted from the source’s experimental interactive concept.", "level": "wild"}]};
